<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warangal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orugallu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Warangal, historically Orugallu, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and was the capital of the Kakatiya dynasty from the late 12th century. Its fort, with great stone gateways, was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> in 1323, recovered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musunuri-nayakas">Musunuri Nayaks</a> in the 1330s and later held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>. It is now one of the largest cities in Telangana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warangal.jpg" alt="Warangal" width="286" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JohnnyBlaze007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WarangalMontage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Warangal lies at an average elevation of 266 metres on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, a landscape of granite rocks and hills. No major river flows near it, so the city relies on the Kakatiya Canal for water. During the 160 years of Kakatiya rule between 1163 and 1323 it was known by names such as Orugallu, Ekashila Nagaram and Omatikonda, all meaning a single stone, in reference to a huge granite boulder in the Warangal fort. Three lakes, Bhadrakali, Dharmasagar and Waddepally, lie around it, with several hills carrying temples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Beta Raja I founded the Kakatiya dynasty, and his son Prola Raja I shifted the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanamkonda">Hanamkonda</a>. Under Ganapathideva it moved from Hanamkonda to Warangal. Later rulers included Rudradeva, Mahadeva, Prataparudra and Rani Rudrama Devi, the only woman to rule the Telugu region. Kakatiya inscriptions praised Warangal as the best city in the Telugu region, up to the shores of the ocean, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> mentioned the dynasty's cultural and administrative distinction. The Kakatiyas left a fortress, four massive stone gateways, the Swayambhu temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and the Ramappa temple near Ramappa Lake.</p><p>In 1323 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> defeated the Kakatiya dynasty, and Juna Khan, crown prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-tughluq">Ghiyath al-Din Tughluq</a>, conquered the city and renamed it Sultanpur. In 1336 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musunuri-nayakas">Musunuri Nayaks</a>, who united 72 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> chieftains, recaptured it, restored the name Orugallu and ruled for fifty years. Warangal then belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>. Aurangzeb conquered Golconda in 1687, and in 1724 the southern provinces split away as the state of Hyderabad, which was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> state, including Warangal, was formed on 2 June 2014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Together with Kazipet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanamkonda">Hanamkonda</a>, Warangal forms the Warangal Tri-City. The Warangal Fort, the Thousand Pillar Temple and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramappa-temple">Ramappa Temple</a> are listed among the World Heritage Sites recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>. The Kakatiya Kala Thoranam, the gateway of the fort, appears in the emblem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>. The Greater Warangal Municipal Corporation, established in 1899, is one of the oldest urban local bodies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The women of the city celebrate the floral festival of Bathukamma, honouring the goddess with flowers for nine days.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Warangal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the east the Bahmanis fought the Musunuri Nayakas and other Telugu chiefs; Muhammad Shah I received the fortress of Golconda and a celebrated turquoise throne from Kapaya Nayaka, and Ahmad Shah I (r. 1422–1436) overran <b>Warangal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He compelled the submission of the Kakatiyas at <b>Warangal</b> (1310), extracted enormous tribute from the Hoysala capital at Dwarasamudra and plundered the Pandya lands around Madurai (1311), so that nearly every major southern Indian power became a tributary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The armies of Alauddin Khalji and Muhammad bin Tughluq had destroyed or humbled the old kingdoms of the Yadavas of Devagiri, the Kakatiyas of <b>Warangal</b> and the Pandyas of Madurai, and in 1327 annexed the small kingdom of Kampili on the Tungabhadra River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning with reinforcements from Delhi after a failed first siege, Ulugh Khan, the future sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq, defeated and captured Prataparudra at the Kakatiya capital, <b>Warangal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>By tradition, Annama Deva, a brother of the last Kakatiya king Prataparudra, fled north after the Delhi Sultanate took <b>Warangal</b> in 1323 and established a kingdom among the Gond and other peoples of the Bastar plateau.</blockquote><a href="/state/bastar-state">Bastar State</a></li><li><blockquote>As Chalukya power declined, Prola II and then Rudradeva asserted independence; Rudradeva declared sovereignty around 1163, built the Thousand Pillar Temple at Hanamkonda and laid out a new capital at <b>Orugallu</b> (Warangal).</blockquote><a href="/state/kakatiya-dynasty">Kakatiya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
